turning now to an important part of our  nation's history a new documentary  series explores president president  abraham lincoln's legacy lincoln's  dilemma is narrated by actor jeffrey  white the four-part series explores the  life and times of president lincoln  through never before heard stories from  historians and scholars and rare  archival material it challenges the  perception of who he was and his complex  journey to end slavery in america this  preview clip examines lincoln's second  inaugural address to the nation  in it and he does this moral calculation  which i think is an astonishing  sentence for  any white person to have said in 1865  all the more so for a head of state  we may have to suffer  as much violence as we have inflicted on  these people  in order to persevere as a nation  and if that happens  the judgments of god are right and just  award-winning journalist jelani cobb is  an executive producer for lincoln's  dilemma he joins us now with historian  and author kelly carter jackson who is  also featured in the series good morning  to you both thank you for joining us  thank you um jelani i'll start with the  beautiful voice by the way  i have to get that out the way beautiful  writer as well yeah and i heard that  while i was watching the doctor's series  um most people think they know president  lincoln's history i mean whether they  were taught it in school or read  afterwards what did this docu-series aim  to set straight well i mean there's so  much about lincoln you just there's  layers and layers and layers and so we  know what we did what he did we don't  necessarily know why he did it or how he  did it or how he came to the decisions  that he made and more importantly we  know very little about the context  generally speaking yeah the context in  which those decisions were made the cast  of characters the different  forces that were seeking to influence  him and particularly the the i think one  of the things we do best in this film is  look at the interplay the relationship  between frederick douglass consistently  pushing abraham lincoln and lincoln  negotiating and moving toward what he  ultimately does in issuing the  emancipation proclamation  yeah i i be in the beginning of this you  you go directly to january 6th and i'm  curious  how you saw that play  a part in this story of lincoln and his  dilemma i mean when you think of the  civil war when you think of the country  at odds  um this is the period in history that  people point to as sort of the precedent  for understanding you know things like  january 6 and civil unrest and political  unrest so there's a lot of resonance  within the documentary and i think the  wonderful thing about history is that  it's such a template or roadmap for  understanding not just where we've been  but where we might go  and i think this documentary does a good  job at showing like what is possible  yeah i hope it doesn't come to a civil  war again absolutely right exactly but  it is a it is a good lesson that we've  been through worse and we can't get  through this uh going back to lincoln  one of the things i thought was  fascinating is the the idea that he's  the great emancipator this was not a  nickname that he welcomed politically  and something you talk about kelly yeah  he is not the great emancipator and he  he pushed back on that idea himself you  know scholars for decades have really  been saying that the enslaved freed  themselves um that they had to get to  union lines they had to run away they  had to take the agency to do it  themselves and so lincoln says over and  over again i did not for you god made  you free yeah um and i think that's so  important because we only sort of know  lincoln by that one-liner lincoln greet  the slaves yeah and it's it's so much  more um complicated and nuanced than  that and lincoln also had some other  ideas after the slaves were freed about  what to do with with integrating the  country joanna you want to take that one  i mean he followed a really torturous  path you know because initially he  believed in in the early versions of the  emancipation proclamation were paired  with this idea that the free black  population would then be  sent out of the country  and they had various ideas about where  this p where these people could go and  people flirted with the idea of sending  all of the freed blacks to haiti uh and  over time and of course this is going  back to the dialogue with frederick  douglass he came comes to understand  that this is not workable and that these  people are a foundational part of what  the united states is when we have to  clear some sort of path for them to be  included there are so many layers to  this docu-series it's beautifully done  and beautifully told  the fate of democracy hanging in the  balance um abraham lincoln trying to  make both sides happy um but i want to  get back to something you said you  mentioned it being a road map  i couldn't help but to draw parallels to  exactly what is going on right now and i  almost thought  we're taking a u-turn because what we  have been going through over the last  couple of years  it's almost like you can take it and put  it over the picture of the past and just  trace it was that a huge part of this  docu-series to remind americans where we  could be headed if we don't find a way  to get on the same page yeah i think you  know not to be alarmist or anything like  that but i do think that the past should  be a cautionary tale for all of us it  should show us  where we have been and how we got down  this horrible road um but also how we  can work to make it better and i think  one of the things that is so great about  lincoln is that he responded to the  people he listened to frederick douglass  he took on you know the wisdom and the  guidance that other people were giving  him to really try to live up to these  ideas of what it means to have an  american democracy and american freedom  for all people yeah  i was just going to say the lincoln of  the debates was it very different  very different  by the time you know of the emancipation  and the end of the civil war  but one of the greatest things about  abraham lincoln is that he evolved with  his time  and there are certainly contradictory  things there are things that he says in  1858 that are outright white supremacist  in those debates with stephen douglas  and  you see him  the reason he's such an intriguing  figure that we've never gotten tired of  talking about him is that he is learning  and evolving and taking in new  information and coming to question his  own assumptions  and it's because he has to do this in  front of an entire nation but i so hope  that that is still possible today  exactly exactly our point that that is  what's most encouraging to me is that  lincoln was responsive to the people on  the ground to the enslaved people  themselves that were literally setting  the agenda i mean frederick douglass  says this is a war of abolition this is  not just about bringing the south back  into the country this is about ending  the institution of slavery born into  slavery and then has the ear of a  president it's an amazing story in its  own right and yeah learning and evolving  is something we all should do uh jelani  cobb and kelly carter jackson thank you  so much thank you the four-part  docu-series lincoln's dilemma premieres  tomorrow on apple tv plus it is great  trust me you should watch it can't wait  to see the rest of it
 

